{"body":"**[major]** **[needs human]** The rate limiters bucket by `req.ip`, and `req.ip` only reflects the real client if the reverse proxy's connection arrives from a loopback address. The repo contains no evidence of the deployment topology (README: the server runs in the `outercolonies_server` container; DB host is a LAN address; no proxy config in the repo): with bridge networking and a published port, or a proxy in a sibling container, the proxy address is NOT loopback, so `X-Forwarded-For` is ignored, every request gets `req.ip` = proxy address and all clients share a single bucket — login would 429 globally after 10 requests/15 min and the register form's exists checks (60/min) would lock all users out together, with no runtime warning since `trust proxy` is truthy. This cannot be verified from the repo: confirm the proxy source address as seen by the server process is loopback (host networking or in-process proxy). If it is not, configure the concrete proxy address instead (e.g. `app.set('trust proxy', '<proxy-ip>')`), and consider a staging smoke test that two distinct client IPs land in distinct buckets.","id":4598685345,"in_reply_to_id":null,"path":"server/src/app.ts","user":"chrthom"}
{"body":"**[nit]** Only request bodies are validated; the `username`/`email` query params of `GET /api/auth/exists` and the `:user` path param of `DELETE /api/auth/password/:user` still pass arbitrary-length values to the database (parameterized, so no injection, but oversized input yields a DB lookup instead of a clean 400). For defense in depth, validate these with the zod schemas too (e.g. reuse the `username`/`email` field schemas for the exists query params).","id":4598685360,"in_reply_to_id":null,"path":"server/src/components/rest_api.ts","user":"chrthom"}
{"body":"**[nit]** Misleading test name: 'should block an eleventh login attempt from a different IP not affected by the limit of another' — the test asserts the opposite (a request from a different IP passes with 200 while another IP exhausted its limit). Rename to e.g. 'should not block a login attempt from an IP whose limit was not exhausted by another client'.","id":4598685370,"in_reply_to_id":null,"path":"server/src/components/rest_api.test.ts","user":"chrthom"}
{"body":"**[nit]** All five invalid-body registration cases share the IP 192.168.1.1 against the real module-level `registerRateLimiter` (10/hour). 5 < 10 today, but extending the it.each matrix will silently trip the limiter and turn expected 400s into 429s. Use a distinct IP per case (as the other tests do) or derive the IP from the case index.","id":4598685375,"in_reply_to_id":null,"path":"server/src/components/rest_api.test.ts","user":"chrthom"}
{"body":"**[nit]** The password strength rule (at least one letter and one digit) now exists twice — here and in `server/src/components/utils/request_validation.ts` — so a future rule change must be made in both places despite the JSDoc cross-references. Consider moving the rule (regex or a shared predicate) into `server/src/shared/` so both the server schema and this validator import the single definition via `@shared/*`.","id":4598685381,"in_reply_to_id":null,"path":"website/src/app/components/password-strength.validator.ts","user":"chrthom"}
{"body":"**[decision]** Confirmed against the production topology (documented in my ops/hosting notes, not in the repo):

- FRITZ!Box port forward 443 → Synology DSM integrated reverse proxy (nginx, "Login Portal → Reverse Proxy") → `outercolonies_server` Docker container (Container Manager).
- The container runs with default **bridge** networking and a published port (the DB host is configured as a LAN address, which confirms bridge rather than host mode).

With bridge networking, the reverse proxy's connection enters the container from the **Docker bridge gateway** (`172.x.x.x`), not from loopback, regardless of the backend hostname configured in DSM. So `trust proxy 'loopback'` would ignore `X-Forwarded-For`, `req.ip` would be the same gateway address for every client, and all users would collapse into one bucket.

**Fix:** change `app.ts` to

```ts
app.set('trust proxy', ['loopback', '172.16.0.0/12']);
```

This covers the default bridge (`172.17.0.1`) and compose networks. The range is not publicly routable, and LAN clients hitting the published port directly (bypassing the proxy) are not in the trusted set, so the spoofing protection is preserved. There is exactly one proxy hop (no CDN; DNS points directly at the connection), so `X-Forwarded-For` is trustworthy once that hop is trusted.

Please keep the staging smoke test: two distinct client IPs must land in distinct buckets (e.g. via the `exists` endpoint or repeated login attempts).","id":4599121997,"in_reply_to_id":4598685345,"path":"server/src/app.ts","user":"chrthom"}
{"body":"dev-loop fixer: changed to app.set('trust proxy', ['loopback', '172.16.0.0/12']) and updated the comment to describe the bridge topology (single trusted proxy hop on the Docker bridge; direct LAN clients bypassing the proxy remain untrusted, so X-Forwarded-For spoofing stays impossible). The staging smoke test (two distinct client IPs must land in distinct buckets, e.g. via the exists endpoint) remains a manual verification at the next staging deployment - the agent cannot run it.","id":4599316121,"in_reply_to_id":4598685345,"path":"server/src/app.ts","user":"chrthom"}
{"body":"dev-loop fixer: added zod validation for the previously unvalidated inputs. The username/email query params of GET /api/auth/exists reuse the username/email field schemas (authExistsQuerySchema), the :user param of DELETE /api/auth/password/:user accepts a username or email (authPasswordResetUserSchema), and the :id params of the activation, password reset and email change endpoints are bounded to the UUID format of the magic links (oneTimeIdSchema). Oversized or malformed values now yield a clean 400 before any database access; covered by new schema and endpoint tests.","id":4599317298,"in_reply_to_id":4598685360,"path":"server/src/components/rest_api.ts","user":"chrthom"}
{"body":"dev-loop fixer: renamed the test to 'should not block a login attempt from an IP whose limit was not exhausted by another client', matching what it asserts.","id":4599318005,"in_reply_to_id":4598685370,"path":"server/src/components/rest_api.test.ts","user":"chrthom"}
{"body":"dev-loop fixer: each invalid-body registration case now sends from its own IP (192.168.1.11 through 192.168.1.15), so extending the it.each matrix can no longer silently trip the module-level registerRateLimiter.","id":4599318760,"in_reply_to_id":4598685375,"path":"server/src/components/rest_api.test.ts","user":"chrthom"}
{"body":"dev-loop fixer: moved the letter+digit rule into server/src/shared/config/password_rules.ts as passwordHasLetterAndDigit; the server's strongPassword schema and the website's passwordStrength validator now both import that single definition (via @shared/config/password_rules), so a future rule change has exactly one place to edit.","id":4599319445,"in_reply_to_id":4598685381,"path":"website/src/app/components/password-strength.validator.ts","user":"chrthom"}
